  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1006 PM CDT Sat Aug 1 2026\n\nWhat a wonderful evening to follow a pleasant day! Skies are\nmostly clear and temperatures hover in the low-to-mid 70s as of\n9pm. Winds have mostly relaxed across the area wit, generally\naround 7 mph or less and will continue to relax to near calm\novernight. This could allow for some patchy fog to develop,\nmainly in low-lying valleys of northeast Nebraska.\n\nSatellite shows the Low continuing to spin to our east over the\nOhio River Valley this evening while ridging builds over the\nNorthern Rockies and the High Plains. As the upper-level trough\ncontinues to drift eastward, we'll lose it's influence and see a\nflow reversal tomorrow. This will bring a wind shift during the\nmorning to weak southerly flow allowing temperatures to warm a\nfew degrees higher than we saw today. Highs on Sunday will be\ngenerally in the mid-to-upper 80s with a few spots closer to\nnorth-central Nebraska peaking in the low 90s. \n\nThis pattern holds into Monday with southerly flow only\namplifying ahead of the trough pushing across the Canadian\nRockies. With this we'll see the warming trend continue with\nhighs forecast to reach the upper 80s to low 90s on Monday. The\ngood news is we're not seeing a lot of humidity advect in with\nthis southerly flow as the source region for this air mass is \nmore over central and west Texas. This should keep humidity and\nheat indices under control, though agricultural contributions\nwill still remain leading to temperatures feeling locally higher\nin some areas.\n\nTuesday we see a more profound impact from the trough moving\nthrough to our north, bringing a cold front into the area\novernight into the early morning hours. Shower and thunderstorm\nchances will return with this front starting in northeast\nNebraska mostly after Midnight early Tuesday in northeast\nNebraska spreading to the I-80 corridor by daybreak. Model\nguidance shows significant spread in the amount of elevated\ninstability present as the front moves through overnight with\na greater amount of bulk shear arriving later in the morning\ncloser to Tuesday afternoon. This should limit severe potential\nwith this first round of storms. Some severe potential seems\nmore possible Tuesday afternoon and evening when model soundings\nand hodographs show a greater combination of instability and\nshear. High storm bases (LCLs) will likely limit tornado\nchances, but we could still see potential for large hail and\ndamaging winds. \n\nTemperatures will be phenomenal on Wednesday with highs mostly \nin the mid 70s. This is due to a Canadian High sliding down the\nRockies into the Central Plains bringing in this cooler air\nmass. It won't be sunshine and roses, though, as we sit on the \nsouthern boundary of this High as it collides with the warmer, \nmoister air mass to the south. This will lead to additional \nshower and storm chances through the day, mostly along and south\nof wherever this frontal boundary situates itself across our \narea.\n\nConfidence drops off in forecast details beyond Wednesday due to\na fairly active, baroclinic pattern setting up with\nnorthwesterly flow bringing in potential weak shortwave\ndisturbances. Temperatures likely rebound going into the end of\nthe week as the Canadian Highs slides off to the east and we see\nthe return of southerly flow going into the weekend. Depending\non how much moisture we advect back into the region, we could\nsee additional shower and storm chances, but again confidence is\nreally low in these details at this time.",
